[00:01.12] By Yehuda Amichai. Translated By Chana Bloch & Stephen Mitchell
[00:09.54] Not the peace of a cease-fire,
[00:12.77] not even the vision of the wolf and the lamb,
[00:16.17] but rather
[00:17.69] as in the heart when the excitement is over
[00:21.38] and you can talk only about a great weariness.
[00:25.83] I know that I know how to kill,
[00:28.42] that makes me an adult.
[00:30.98] And my son plays with a toy gun that knows
[00:34.59] how to open and close its eyes and say Mama.
[00:38.81] A peace
[00:39.60] without the big noise of beating swords into ploughshares,
[00:44.37] without words, without
[00:46.83] the thud of the heavy rubber stamp: let it be
[00:50.66] light, floating, like lazy white foam.
[00:54.92] A little rest for the wounds—
[00:57.25] who speaks of healing?
[00:59.45] (And the howl of the orphans is passed from one generation
[01:03.58] to the next, as in a relay race:
[01:06.57] the baton never falls.)
[01:08.96] Let it come
[01:10.68] like wildflowers,
[01:12.26] suddenly, because the field
[01:15.12] must have it: wildpeace.
[01:18.22] 